cgrun057: the flag -xc requires the program to be built with -prof
cgrun057: 
cgrun057: Usage: <prog> <args> [+RTS <rtsopts> | -RTS <args>] ... --RTS <args>
cgrun057: 
cgrun057:    +RTS     Indicates run time system options follow
cgrun057:    -RTS     Indicates program arguments follow
cgrun057:   --RTS     Indicates that ALL subsequent arguments will be given to the
cgrun057:             program (including any of these RTS flags)
cgrun057: 
cgrun057: The following run time system options may be available (note that some
cgrun057: of these may not be usable unless this program was linked with the -rtsopts
cgrun057: flag):
cgrun057: 
cgrun057:   -?        Prints this message and exits; the program is not executed
cgrun057:   --info    Print information about the RTS used by this program
cgrun057: 
cgrun057:   --nonmoving-gc
cgrun057:             Selects the non-moving mark-and-sweep garbage collector to
cgrun057:             manage the oldest generation.
cgrun057:   --copying-gc
cgrun057:             Selects the copying garbage collector to manage all generations.
cgrun057: 
cgrun057:   -K<size>  Sets the maximum stack size (default: 80% of the heap)
cgrun057:             e.g.: -K32k -K512k -K8M
cgrun057:   -ki<size> Sets the initial thread stack size (default 1k)  e.g.: -ki4k -ki2m
cgrun057:   -kc<size> Sets the stack chunk size (default 32k)
cgrun057:   -kb<size> Sets the stack chunk buffer size (default 1k)
cgrun057: 
cgrun057:   -A<size>  Sets the minimum allocation area size (default 4m) e.g.: -A20m -A10k
cgrun057:   -AL<size> Sets the amount of large-object memory that can be allocated
cgrun057:             before a GC is triggered (default: the value of -A)
cgrun057:   -F<n>     Sets the collecting threshold for old generations as a factor of
cgrun057:             the live data in that generation the last time it was collected
cgrun057:             (default: 2.0)
cgrun057:   -Fd<n>    Sets the inverse rate which memory is returned to the OS after being
cgrun057:             optimistically retained after being allocated. Subsequent major
cgrun057:             collections not caused by heap overflow will return an amount of
cgrun057:             memory controlled by this factor (higher is slower). Setting the factor
cgrun057:             to 0 means memory is not returned.
cgrun057:             (default 4.0)
cgrun057:   -n<size>  Allocation area chunk size (0 = disabled, default: 0)
cgrun057:   -O<size>  Sets the minimum size of the old generation (default 1M)
cgrun057:   -M<size>  Sets the maximum heap size (default unlimited)  e.g.: -M256k -M1G
cgrun057:   -H<size>  Sets the minimum heap size (default 0M)   e.g.: -H24m  -H1G
cgrun057:   -xb<addr> Sets the address from which a suitable start for the heap memory
cgrun057:             will be searched from. This is useful if the default address
cgrun057:             clashes with some third-party library.
cgrun057:   -xn       Use the non-moving collector for the old generation.
cgrun057:   -m<n>     Minimum % of heap which must be available (default 3%)
cgrun057:   -G<n>     Number of generations (default: 2)
cgrun057:   -c<n>     Use in-place compaction instead of copying in the oldest generation
cgrun057:             when live data is at least <n>% of the maximum heap size set with
cgrun057:             -M (default: 30%)
cgrun057:   -c        Use in-place compaction for all oldest generation collections
cgrun057:             (the default is to use copying)
cgrun057:   -w        Use mark-region for the oldest generation (experimental)
cgrun057: 
cgrun057:   -T         Collect GC statistics (useful for in-program statistics access)
cgrun057:   -t[<file>] One-line GC statistics (if <file> omitted, uses stderr)
cgrun057:   -s[<file>] Summary  GC statistics (if <file> omitted, uses stderr)
cgrun057:   -S[<file>] Detailed GC statistics (if <file> omitted, uses stderr)
cgrun057: 
cgrun057: 
cgrun057:   -Z         Don't squeeze out update frames on context switch
cgrun057:   -B         Sound the bell at the start of each garbage collection
cgrun057:   -h       Heap residency profile (output file <program>.hp)
cgrun057:   -hT      Produce a heap profile grouped by closure type
cgrun057:   -hi      Produce a heap profile grouped by info table address
cgrun057:   -po<file>  Override profiling output file name prefix (program name by default)
cgrun057:   -i<sec>  Time between heap profile samples (seconds, default: 0.1)
cgrun057:   --no-automatic-heap-samples
cgrun057:            Do not start the heap profile interval timer on start-up,
cgrun057:            Rather, the application will be responsible for triggering
cgrun057:            heap profiler samples.
cgrun057:   -ol<file>  Send binary eventlog to <file> (default: <program>.eventlog)
cgrun057:   -l[flags]  Log events to a file
cgrun057:              where [flags] can contain:
cgrun057:                 s    scheduler events
cgrun057:                 g    GC and heap events
cgrun057:                 n    non-moving GC heap census events
cgrun057:                 p    par spark events (sampled)
cgrun057:                 f    par spark events (full detail)
cgrun057:                 u    user events (emitted from Haskell code)
cgrun057:                 a    all event classes above
cgrun057:                -x    disable an event class, for any flag above
cgrun057:              the initial enabled event classes are 'sgpu'
cgrun057:  --eventlog-flush-interval=<secs>
cgrun057:              Periodically flush the eventlog at the specified interval.
cgrun057: 
cgrun057:   -C<secs>  Context-switch interval in seconds.
cgrun057:             0 or no argument means switch as often as possible.
cgrun057:             Default: 0.02 sec.
cgrun057:   -V<secs>  Master tick interval in seconds (0 == disable timer).
cgrun057:             This sets the resolution for -C and the heap profile timer -i,
cgrun057:             and is the frequency of time profile samples.
cgrun057:             Default: 0.01 sec.
cgrun057: 
cgrun057:   --install-signal-handlers=<yes|no>
cgrun057:              Install signal handlers (default: yes)
cgrun057:   --io-manager=<name>
cgrun057:              The I/O manager to use.
cgrun057:              Options available: auto select (default: select)
cgrun057:   -xp        Assume that all object files were compiled with -fPIC
cgrun057:              -fexternal-dynamic-refs and load them anywhere in the address
cgrun057:              space
cgrun057:   -xm        Base address to mmap memory in the GHCi linker
cgrun057:              (hex; must be <80000000)
cgrun057:   -xq        The allocation limit given to a thread after it receives
cgrun057:              an AllocationLimitExceeded exception. (default: 100k)
cgrun057: 
cgrun057:   -xr        The size of virtual memory address space reserved by the
cgrun057:              two step allocator (default: 1T)
cgrun057: 
cgrun057:   -Mgrace=<n>
cgrun057:              The amount of allocation after the program receives a
cgrun057:              HeapOverflow exception before the exception is thrown again, if
cgrun057:              the program is still exceeding the heap limit.
cgrun057: 
cgrun057:   --read-tix-file=<yes|no>
cgrun057:              Whether to initialize HPC datastructures from  <program>.tix              at the start of execution. (default: yes)
cgrun057: 
cgrun057:   --write-tix-file=<yes|no>
cgrun057:              Whether to write <program>.tix at the end of execution.
cgrun057:              (default: yes)
cgrun057: 
cgrun057: RTS options may also be specified using the GHCRTS environment variable.
cgrun057: 
cgrun057: Other RTS options may be available for programs compiled a different way.
cgrun057: The GHC User's Guide has full details.
